  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Useful as a carrier-neutral envelope boundary: it can move opaque payload bytes across many substrates and makes unsupported interpretation explicit, but it only indirectly addresses chunk-feed replication and unsigned v0 leaves trust/accountability to the carrier or payload layer.",
    "strengths": [
      "Simple [pcid,payload] DAG-CBOR envelope is easy to carry over UDP, git, libp2p, IPFS, and similar substrates.",
      "Ordering, forwarding, and body-reference rules are explicitly pushed above the envelope layer, matching the scenario's feed-vs-carrier split.",
      "Unknown-pCID best-effort handling supports sparse knowledge without requiring a central registry."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "It does not define feed/chunk replication behavior, so scenario coverage is only indirect.",
      "Unsigned v0 offers no carrier-independent integrity, authorship, or accountability.",
      "Draft status and no frozen pCID/spec identity weaken 100-year durability and migration confidence."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Carrier-specific trust or metadata can leak into feed meaning, undermining true substrate independence.",
      "Best-effort salvage of unknown payload bytes may be treated as more trustworthy than it is.",
      "Tampering, replay, and attribution disputes are hard to resolve without envelope-level signatures."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Where do chunk identity, sequencing, and replication proofs live if not in the envelope?",
      "What local records let Alice or Carol audit a cross-carrier exchange without trusting the carrier?",
      "What is the migration path from unsigned v0 to a signed successor without carrier-specific breakage?",
      "How are long-term pCID discovery and handler availability kept decentralized?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
